terpart of [`/stock/:ticker/greek-flow/:expiry`](https://api.unusualwhales.com/docs/operations/PublicApi.TickerController.greek_flow_expiry),
which returns the same fields with the same encodings. Note the REST endpoint serves each
minute's **accumulated total**, while this channel serves the per-second partials that add up
to it.

Payload format for `greek_flow_expiry:<TICKER>`:

```
[
  "greek_flow_expiry:SPY",
  {
    "ticker": "SPY",
    "timestamp": "2026-09-04T15:46:00Z",
    "expiry": "2026-10-16",
    "total_delta_flow": "-21257.36",
    "dir_delta_flow": "-43593.96",
    "otm_total_delta_flow": "-28564.02",
    "otm_dir_delta_flow": "14947.51",
    "total_vega_flow": "350944.58",
    "dir_vega_flow": "31243.04",
    "otm_total_vega_flow": "101745.64",
    "otm_dir_vega_flow": "11421.03",
    "transactions": 77,
    "volume": 4242
  }
]
```

### Field reference

| Field                  | Type    | Description |
|------------------------|---------|-------------|
| `ticker`               | string  | Root ticker of the underlying. |
| `timestamp`            | string  | The minute bucket these trades fall into, ISO 8601 UTC. |
| `expiry`               | string  | The option expiry this bucket is broken out by, `YYYY-MM-DD`. |
| `total_delta_flow`     | string  | Sum of signed delta exposure: `delta * contracts * 100`. |
| `dir_delta_flow`       | string  | Delta magnitude signed by sentiment: positive for bullish trades (buy call / sell put), negative for bearish. |
| `otm_total_delta_flow` | string  | `total_delta_flow` counting only trades identified as out of the money. |
| `otm_dir_delta_flow`   | string  | `dir_delta_flow` counting only trades identified as out of the money. |
| `total_vega_flow`      | string  | Sum of vega exposure: `vega * contracts * 100`. |
| `dir_vega_flow`        | string  | Vega magnitude signed by side: positive for buys (ask side), negative for sells (bid side). |
| `otm_total_vega_flow`  | string  | `total_vega_flow` counting only trades identified as out of the money. |
| `otm_dir_vega_flow`    | string  | `dir_vega_flow` counting only trades identified as out of the money. |
| `transactions`         | integer | Number of option trades aggregated into this message. A trade whose delta or vega was unavailable still counts here and in `volume` but adds zero to that greek's flow fields. |
| `volume`               | integer | Total contracts traded across the aggregated trades. |


### Fully resolved operation
```json
{
  "key": "GET /api/socket/greek_flow_expiry",
  "method": "GET",
  "path": "/api/socket/greek_flow_expiry",
  "transport": "websocket-documentation",
  "operation": {
    "callbacks": {},
    "description": "**NOTE:**\nThis is the documentation for the websocket channels `greek_flow_expiry` and\n`greek_flow_expiry:<TICKER>`.\nFor the same data aggregated across all expiries, see [`greek_flow`](https://api.unusualwhales.com/docs/operations/PublicApi.SocketController.greek_flow).\nWebsocket access for personal use is only available through the [Advanced plan](https://unusualwhales.com/pricing?product=api).\n\nYou can find fully-functional examples that stream data from many channels here:\n\n- Python: [https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output](https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output)\n- Javascript: [https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output-nodejs](https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output-nodejs)\n\n\n\nConnect to the websocket URI:\n\n`wss://api.unusualwhales.com/socket?token=<YOUR_API_TOKEN>`\n\nthen `join` the channel you wish to stream, for example `greek_flow_expiry:SPY` for SPY's\ndelta and vega flow broken out per option expiry. Omit the ticker suffix to receive updates\nfor every ticker.\n\nEach message carries the delta and vega exposure of the option trades that printed for one\nticker, in one minute, **for a single option expiry**, and arrived since the previous message\nfor that minute and expiry; see Delivery characteristics below for how to sum them. Both\nchannels are built from the same option trades, so once every message for a minute has\narrived, that minute's expiries sum to the same minute on the\n[`greek_flow`](https://api.unusualwhales.com/docs/operations/PublicApi.SocketController.greek_flow)\nchannel. `total_*` fields carry the greek's own sign - for delta that means calls contribute\npositive and puts negative - so they are net greek exposure regardless of who was the\naggressor. `dir_*` fields instead sign the magnitude by trade sentiment: for delta, bullish\ntrades (bought calls, sold puts) positive and bearish negative; for vega, buys (ask side)\npositive and sells (bid side) negative. Mid/no-side trades are excluded from every `dir_*`\nfield.\n\n### Delivery characteristics\n\nRead these before writing a client, they are not obvious from the payload:\n\n- **Each message is a partial sum, not a running total.** A minute's bucket is flushed\n  roughly once per second as trades arrive, so to get that minute's total for one expiry you\n  must **add together every message sharing the same `ticker`, `timestamp` and `expiry`**.\n  Grouping on `timestamp` alone is wrong even on the per-ticker channel, since every expiry\n  that traded carries that same minute; on the global channel every ticker does too. A client\n  that overwrites on the key instead of adding will silently under-report.\n- Because of that, a dropped frame does not just delay a value, it changes your sum. Read\n  from the socket promptly: a slow consumer is not disconnected, but frames it fails to keep\n  up with are dropped.\n- **Updates are trade-driven, not scheduled.** An expiry with no option trades in the window\n  produces no message at all - there is no per-minute tick and no zero-valued heartbeat. A\n  minute bucket is also never announced as closed: messages carrying a given `timestamp`\n  simply stop arriving.\n- **This channel carries considerably more messages than `greek_flow`** - one per expiry that\n  traded, rather than one per ticker.\n- The channels are keyed by the **root** ticker: weekly/PM index series are folded into their\n  root (`SPXW` under `SPX`, `NDXP` under `NDX`, `VIXW` under `VIX`, `RUTW` under `RUT`).\n  Joining `greek_flow_expiry:SPXW` is acknowledged as, and streams, `greek_flow_expiry:SPX`.\n- All flow values are decimal strings, matching the REST endpoint below.\n\nThis is the live counterpart of [`/stock/:ticker/greek-flow/:expiry`](https://api.unusualwhales.com/docs/operations/PublicApi.TickerController.greek_flow_expiry),\nwhich returns the same fields with the same encodings. Note the REST endpoint serves each\nminute's **accumulated total**, while this channel serves the per-second partials that add up\nto it.\n\nPayload format for `greek_flow_expiry:<TICKER>`:\n\n```\n[\n  \"greek_flow_expiry:SPY\",\n  {\n    \"ticker\": \"SPY\",\n    \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-04T15:46:00Z\",\n    \"expiry\": \"2026-10-16\",\n    \"total_delta_flow\": \"-21257.36\",\n    \"dir_delta_flow\": \"-43593.96\",\n    \"otm_total_delta_flow\": \"-28564.02\",\n    \"otm_dir_delta_flow\": \"14947.51\",\n    \"total_vega_flow\": \"350944.58\",\n    \"dir_vega_flow\": \"31243.04\",\n    \"otm_total_vega_flow\": \"101745.64\",\n    \"otm_dir_vega_flow\": \"11421.03\",\n    \"transactions\": 77,\n    \"volume\": 4242\n  }\n]\n```\n\n### Field reference\n\n| Field                  | Type    | Description |\n|------------------------|---------|-------------|\n| `ticker`               | string  | Root ticker of the underlying. |\n| `timestamp`            | string  | The minute bucket these trades fall into, ISO 8601 UTC. |\n| `expiry`               | string  | The option expiry this bucket is broken out by, `YYYY-MM-DD`. |\n| `total_delta_flow`     | string  | Sum of signed delta exposure: `delta * contracts * 100`. |\n| `dir_delta_flow`       | string  | Delta magnitude signed by sentiment: positive for bullish trades (buy call / sell put), negative for bearish. |\n| `otm_total_delta_flow` | string  | `total_delta_flow` counting only trades identified as out of the money. |\n| `otm_dir_delta_flow`   | string  | `dir_delta_flow` counting only trades identified as out of the money. |\n| `total_vega_flow`      | string  | Sum of vega exposure: `vega * contracts * 100`. |\n| `dir_vega_flow`        | string  | Vega magnitude signed by side: positive for buys (ask side), negative for sells (bid side). |\n| `otm_total_vega_flow`  | string  | `total_vega_flow` counting only trades identified as out of the money. |\n| `otm_dir_vega_flow`    | string  | `dir_vega_flow` counting only trades identified as out of the money. |\n| `transactions`         | integer | Number of option trades aggregated into this message. A trade whose delta or vega was unavailable still counts here and in `volume` but adds zero to that greek's flow fields. |\n| `volume`               | integer | Total contracts traded across the aggregated trades. |\n",
    "operationId": "PublicApi.SocketController.greek_flow_expiry",
    "parameters": [],
    "responses": {
      "200": {
        "content": {
          "application/json": {
            "schema": {
              "type": "object"
            }
          }
        },
        "description": ""
      }
    },
    "summary": "Greek flow by expiry",
    "tags": [
      "websocket"
    ],
    "security": [
      {
        "authorization": []
      }
    ],
    "servers": [
      {
        "url": "https://api.unusualwhales.com",
        "variables": {}
      }
    ]
  }
}

```

## GET /api/socket/greeks

Greeks

**NOTE:**
This is the documentation for websocket channels `greeks` and `greeks:<TICKER>`.
Websocket access for personal use is only available through the [Advanced plan](https://unusualwhales.com/pricing?product=api).

You can find fully-functional examples that stream data from many channels here:

- Python: [https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output](https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output)
- Javascript: [https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output-nodejs](https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output-nodejs)



Connect to the websocket URI:

`wss://api.unusualwhales.com/socket?token=<YOUR_API_TOKEN>`

then `join` the channel you wish to stream: `greeks` for every underlying at once,
or `greeks:SPY` for a single one.

Both channels stream the live counterpart of [/api/stock/{ticker}/greeks](https://api.unusualwhales.com/docs#/operations/PublicApi.TickerController.greeks),
with a different shape: the REST endpoint returns one row per strike for a single
expiry with `call_*`/`put_*` field pairs, while these channels stream **one message
per contract**, across all expiries, with a single set of greek fields plus
`option_type`. A message is emitted every time a contract's greeks are recalculated
and its theoretical price moved - for a busy underlying that is many messages per
second across the whole chain.

Weekly/PM index series are folded into their canonical root on both channels:
`greeks:SPX` includes SPXW contracts (likewise VIXW under VIX, NDXP under NDX, RUTW
under RUT). The `ticker` field carries the canonical root; use `option_symbol` to
tell the series apart.

Payload format:

Format for `greeks:<TICKER>`:

```
[
  "greeks:SPY",
  {
    "option_symbol": "SPY261218C00480000",
    "ticker": "SPY",
    "expiry": "2026-12-18",
    "strike": "480",
    "option_type": "call",
    "timestamp": 1726670396000,
    "volatility": 0.2543,
    "delta": 0.5231,
    "gamma": 0.0125,
    "theta": -0.0821,
    "vega": 1.2143,
    "rho": 0.4312,
    "theo": 38.5217,
    "vanna": 0.1123,
    "vomma": 2.3121,
    "veta": -1.0212,
    "speed": -0.0031,
    "zomma": 0.0523,
    "color": -0.0012,
    "ultima": 3.1214,
    "charm": -0.0214
  }
]
```

The global `greeks` channel streams the same per-contract object for every
underlying, tagged `greeks` (the `ticker` field distinguishes them):

```
[
  "greeks",
  {
    "option_symbol": "AAPL261218C00250000",
    "ticker": "AAPL",
    "expiry": "2026-12-18",
    "strike": "250",
    "option_type": "call",
    "timestamp": 1726670396000,
    "volatility": 0.3121,
    "delta": 0.4812,
    "gamma": 0.0214,
    "theta": -0.0712,
    "vega": 0.8123,
    "rho": 0.2145,
    "theo": 14.2317,
    "vanna": 0.0912,
    "vomma": 1.7213,
    "veta": -0.8121,
    "speed": -0.0021,
    "zomma": 0.0312,
    "color": -0.0009,
    "ultima": 2.1123,
    "charm": -0.0118
  }
]
```

**The global `greeks` channel is a firehose.** Every contract of every optionable
underlying is repriced continuously, so this channel is in the same volume class as
`option_trades` and `gex_strike_expiry` - tens of thousands of messages per second
at peak. Prefer `greeks:<TICKER>` unless you genuinely need the whole chain, and if
you do subscribe globally, read the socket on a dedicated task and buffer/flush
rather than processing inline: a client that cannot keep up will have frames
dropped.

Note that unlike the `gex` and `periscope` channels, the greek values on these
channels are JSON **numbers**, not decimal strings: they are floating point
quantities as computed, so you may see full double precision (e.g.
`0.30000000000000004`). Round on your side to whatever precision you display.

Fields:

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `option_symbol` | string | OSI option symbol; the contract's primary identifier. Keeps the raw series root (e.g. `SPXW...` under `SPX`). |
| `ticker` | string | Canonical underlying root. On `greeks:<TICKER>` it matches the channel key; on the global `greeks` channel it identifies the underlying. |
| `expiry` | date string `YYYY-MM-DD` | Contract expiration. |
| `strike` | decimal string | Strike price. |
| `option_type` | string | `call` or `put`. |
| `timestamp` | int (ms) | Calculation time, unix epoch milliseconds. |
| `volatility` | number | Implied volatility. |
| `delta`, `gamma`, `theta`, `vega`, `rho` | number | First-order greeks. |
| `theo` | number | Theoretical price of the contract. |
| `vanna`, `vomma`, `veta`, `speed`, `zomma`, `color`, `ultima`, `charm` | number | Second-order greeks. |


### Fully resolved operation
```json
{
  "key": "GET /api/socket/greeks",
  "method": "GET",
  "path": "/api/socket/greeks",
  "transport": "websocket-documentation",
  "operation": {
    "callbacks": {},
    "description": "**NOTE:**\nThis is the documentation for websocket channels `greeks` and `greeks:<TICKER>`.\nWebsocket access for personal use is only available through the [Advanced plan](https://unusualwhales.com/pricing?product=api).\n\nYou can find fully-functional examples that stream data from many channels here:\n\n- Python: [https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output](https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output)\n- Javascript: [https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output-nodejs](https://github.com/unusual-whales/api-examples/tree/main/examples/ws-multi-channel-multi-output-nodejs)\n\n\n\nConnect to the websocket URI:\n\n`wss://api.unusualwhales.com/socket?token=<YOUR_API_TOKEN>`\n\nthen `join` the channel you wish to stream: `greeks` for every underlying at once,\nor `greeks:SPY` for a single one.\n\nBoth channels stream the live counterpart of [/api/stock/{ticker}/greeks](https://api.unusualwhales.com/docs#/operations/PublicApi.TickerController.greeks),\nwith a different shape: the REST endpoint returns one row per strike for a single\nexpiry with `call_*`/`put_*` field pairs, while these channels stream **one message\nper contract**, across all expiries, with a single set of greek fields plus\n`option_type`. A message is emitted every time a contract's greeks are recalculated\nand its theoretical price moved - for a busy underlying that is many messages per\nsecond across the whole chain.\n\nWeekly/PM index series are folded into their canonical root on both channels:\n`greeks:SPX` includes SPXW contracts (likewise VIXW under VIX, NDXP under NDX, RUTW\nunder RUT). The `ticker` field carries the canonical root; use `option_symbol` to\ntell the series apart.\n\nPayload format:\n\nFormat for `greeks:<TICKER>`:\n\n```\n[\n  \"greeks:SPY\",\n  {\n    \"option_symbol\": \"SPY261218C00480000\",\n    \"ticker\": \"SPY\",\n    \"expiry\": \"2026-12-18\",\n    \"strike\": \"480\",\n    \"option_type\": \"call\",\n    \"timestamp\": 1726670396000,\n    \"volatility\": 0.2543,\n    \"delta\": 0.5231,\n    \"gamma\": 0.0125,\n    \"theta\": -0.0821,\n    \"vega\": 1.2143,\n    \"rho\": 0.4312,\n    \"theo\": 38.5217,\n    \"vanna\": 0.1123,\n    \"vomma\": 2.3121,\n    \"veta\": -1.0212,\n    \"speed\": -0.0031,\n    \"zomma\": 0.0523,\n    \"color\": -0.0012,\n    \"ultima\": 3.1214,\n    \"charm\": -0.0214\n  }\n]\n```\n\nThe global `greeks` channel streams the same per-contract object for every\nunderlying, tagged `greeks` (the `ticker` field distinguishes them):\n\n```\n[\n  \"greeks\",\n  {\n    \"option_symbol\": \"AAPL261218C00250000\",\n    \"ticker\": \"AAPL\",\n    \"expiry\": \"2026-12-18\",\n    \"strike\": \"250\",\n    \"option_type\": \"call\",\n    \"timestamp\": 1726670396000,\n    \"volatility\": 0.3121,\n    \"delta\": 0.4812,\n    \"gamma\": 0.0214,\n    \"theta\": -0.0712,\n    \"vega\": 0.8123,\n    \"rho\": 0.2145,\n    \"theo\": 14.2317,\n    \"vanna\": 0.0912,\n    \"vomma\": 1.7213,\n    \"veta\": -0.8121,\n    \"speed\": -0.0021,\n    \"zomma\": 0.0312,\n    \"color\": -0.0009,\n    \"ultima\": 2.1123,\n    \"charm\": -0.0118\n  }\n]\n```\n\n**The global `greeks` channel is a firehose.** Every contract of every optionable\nunderlying is repriced continuously, so this channel is in the same volume class as\n`option_trades` and `gex_strike_expiry` - tens of thousands of messages per second\nat peak. Prefer `greeks:<TICKER>` unless you genuinely need the whole chain, and if\nyou do subscribe globally, read the socket on a dedicated task and buffer/flush\nrather than processing inline: a client that cannot keep up will have frames\ndropped.\n\nNote that unlike the `gex` and `periscope` channels, the greek values on these\nchannels are JSON **numbers**, not decimal strings: they are floating point\nquantities as computed, so you may see full double precision (e.g.\n`0.30000000000000004`). Round on your side to whatever precision you display.\n\nFields:\n\n| Field | Type | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `option_symbol` | string | OSI option symbol; the contract's primary identifier. Keeps the raw series root (e.g. `SPXW...` under `SPX`). |\n| `ticker` | string | Canonical underlying root. On `greeks:<TICKER>` it matches the channel key; on the global `greeks` channel it identifies the underlying. |\n| `expiry` | date string `YYYY-MM-DD` | Contract expiration. |\n| `strike` | decimal string | Strike price. |\n| `option_type` | string | `call` or `put`. |\n| `timestamp` | int (ms) | Calculation time, unix epoch milliseconds. |\n| `volatility` | number | Implied volatility. |\n| `delta`, `gamma`, `theta`, `vega`, `rho` | number | First-order greeks. |\n| `theo` | number | Theoretical price of the contract. |\n| `vanna`, `vomma`, `veta`, `speed`, `zomma`, `color`, `ultima`, `charm` | number | Second-order greeks. |\n",
    "operationId": "PublicApi.SocketController.greeks",
    "parameters": [],
    "responses": {
      "200": {
        "content": {
          "application/json": {
            "schema": {
              "type": "object"
            }
          }
        },
        "description": ""
      }
    },
    "summary": "Greeks",
    "tags": [
      "websocket"
    ],
